===========================================================================
3. Registry of VUURWERK-original features
===========================================================================

VUURWERK-original contributions (designed and conceived by James Honiball).

  THE FOLLOWING ITEMS FALL UNDER COMMERCIAL LICENSING. Outside the
  complete VUURWERK firmware package, no item on this list may be used,
  copied, extracted, ported, re-implemented from this description, or
  incorporated into any other project or product without a commercial
  license granted in writing by James Honiball (KC3TFZ). Within the
  complete VUURWERK firmware they are licensed under GPL v3 as a package.
  See the Dual-Licensing Notice below for the full terms.

  Each entry names the feature, says what it does for the operator, and
  records the version that introduced or changed it where the record
  holds one. Retired features stay listed as the record of the original
  work.

  - TX Audio Compressor: Real-time dynamic microphone compression that
    catches a sudden shout at once and eases back smoothly, so your voice
    reaches the other end at an even level. No other UV-K5 firmware has
    dynamic TX audio processing.

  - TX Soft Start (S-Curve): A smooth, shaped transmitter power ramp on
    key-up that removes the key-up click.

  - CTCSS Lead-In: A brief microphone mute at the start of each
    transmission so the sub-audible tone reaches full level before your
    voice begins, preventing a repeater from clipping your first syllable.
    No other UV-K5 firmware does this.

  - Intelligence-Based Squelch: Combines signal strength, noise and
    interference readings into one voice score instead of three fixed
    thresholds. Extended in v1.3.1: a parked radio opens slightly earlier
    for a likely voice once it has learned the channel's noise floor.

  - Squelch Tail Elimination (Universal): Mutes the squelch tail on CTCSS
    and DCS channels with one switch (Menu > TONE > STE), on any brand of
    repeater regardless of its own tail-elimination method. Extended to
    DCS in v1.3.1, hardened in v1.3.3 against a co-channel signal on a
    non-matching tone, and in v1.5.1 the CTCSS tail mute follows the
    receiver's own tone detector.

  - FM Gain Staging: Adaptive front-end gain control for FM that adapts
    the receiver to signal strength, ramping up to find weak signals and
    pulling back on strong ones. No other UV-K5 firmware has adaptive gain
    control for FM. Since v1.5.1 dual watch, USB and VFO Split no longer
    upset it.

  - S-Meter Compensation: Keeps the S-meter true to the real signal
    strength whatever the receiver gain is doing; the FM counterpart of
    the AM Fix correction.

  - Unified Calibrated Signal Readout: One shared, per-band calibrated
    figure (v1.3.4) feeds both the RX line ("RX S7 -83dBm Q3", its
    trailing class letter retired in v1.5.1) and the centre signal bar, so
    every on-screen signal indication agrees.

  - VOX Voice-Seeking Spectrum (evolution v1.2.0 through v1.5.1): A
    spectrum analyzer mode built on VUURWERK's voice scoring that shows
    and hunts voices rather than raw signals. Original constituent works:
      * Per-bin voice scoring: every bin is scored for voice at the end of
        each sweep; only voice bins draw a bar (half height for a weaker
        score). No other UV-K5 firmware scores spectrum bins for voice.
      * Directional voice seek (v1.3.5): UP walks to the next voice bin
        above the cursor, DOWN below, no wrap; with no voice that way the
        key moves the span instead, so a held key patrols the band and
        stops on voice.
      * Voice gate auto-listen and hold (v1.3.3): when the strongest bin
        is not a voice, the auto-listen picks the strongest real voice bin
        instead; it rides through natural speech gaps and lets go about
        0.6 s after the voice ends.
      * PTT voice lock and open-squelch camp (v1.3.6): PTT while hearing a
        voice locks onto that frequency ("LOCKED"), camping as an
        open-squelch monitor that never resumes the hunt by itself; UP or
        DOWN releases it.
      * VFO frequency carry-over (v1.3.6, re-engineered v1.4.2): leaving
        the spectrum while locked writes the locked frequency into the
        active VFO, in frequency mode on the VFO's own band only, rounded
        to the VFO's step and skipped when reverse is on (v1.4.5); memory
        channels are never written. Since v1.5.1 the modulation heard is
        carried too.
      * Deterministic spectrum exit (v1.4.3, rebuilt v1.5.1): every exit
        returns the radio to your own VFO, hearing normally, with Monitor
        restored if it was on; a PTT or EXIT still held at exit is
        ignored, and entry is refused while the keypad is locked.
      * Unattended PATROL (v1.5.1): a hands-free form of the VOX voice
        hunt. In VOX, 2 sets the range start ("SET LO") and 8 the stop
        ("SET HI") from the window on screen; the range, step and
        modulation are saved on the radio. 0 starts and stops PATROL and
        the corner label reads PTRL while it runs. The range is walked
        window by window, lap after lap, with the last window aligned so
        the whole range is covered. Unattended safeguards: no automatic
        stop lasts longer than 60 seconds, after which that frequency is
        skipped for about 2 minutes of sweeping; MENU while stopped on a
        signal skips that frequency until PATROL stops; a PTT lock is
        never cut off; PATROL stops and leaves the spectrum when the
        battery reaches 5 percent so the radio's own low-battery warning
        can show; and the BackLt timeout applies while it runs. Four menu
        settings (PSens, PSpeed, PDwell, PResum in Menu > SCAN) tune the
        run, and all four on NORM reproduce the hand-held hunt.

  - Scan+Watch: Scanning one VFO while watching the other for activity.
    Original operational concept not present in any upstream firmware.
    The setting stays armed across scan stops (v1.3.0); a find on the
    watched VFO never disturbs the scan (v1.2.5).

  - Adaptive Energy-Gated Scan Dwell (v1.3.3): Scanning skips empty
    channels almost instantly and lingers only where there is signal,
    roughly two to three times faster on an empty band with no loss of
    weak-signal sensitivity.

  - Coded-Channel Scan Dwell (v1.3.3): Tone-coded (CTCSS/DCS) memory
    channels get a longer scan look so the decoder can confirm the tone
    before the scanner steps on, so coded channels are no longer skipped.
    Developed from a field report by Scott Savage (KC7WDG).

  - F+EXIT Reset-to-Simplex Rescue (v1.4.4, armed v1.4.5): One gesture
    that returns the current VFO to clean simplex: tones off, offset off,
    reverse off, scrambler off, FM. It is saved like a keyed-in frequency;
    memory channels are never touched ("FREQ ONLY" toast). The one-gesture
    cure for a polluted VFO.

  - Small-Font Renderer Clamp (v1.3.3): A display-drawing safety fix that
    prevents a corrupted screen from corrupting settings.

  - Menu Hint Line (v1.3.1): Plain-language descriptions on the menu's
    bottom line while an item is highlighted, without renaming or
    reordering any stock menu item. Since v1.5.1 the four PATROL settings
    share one "PATROL ONLY" hint.

  - VFO Split: Independent TX/RX frequency operation that checks VFO B for
    activity while you listen on VFO A. Extended (v1.3.1) with an "SP"
    status-bar indicator and an "ACT <frequency>" alert toast plus a
    single beep when a walked channel comes alive. Since v1.5.1 the alert
    fires once each time a channel goes from quiet to active, and the
    check stands aside for scans and Monitor.

  - Bandscope on Main Screen: Background RF activity display on the main
    screen's centre row during normal operation, inspired by the Icom
    IC-7300's signature feature and drawn in 8 pixels of height on a $30
    radio, without interrupting audio.

  - Intelligent Dual-Watch: Activity-weighted dwell for dual watch, so a
    busy VFO is checked less often and a long conversation outweighs a
    one-second carrier blip (v1.2.7); faster detection of a call on the
    other VFO since v1.3.1.

  - Activity Log: A 20-entry record of RF activity (frequency, strength,
    tone, duration) when the squelch opens, plus radio uptime. Retired in
    v1.2.5 and no longer in the firmware; listed as the record of the
    original work.

  - Signal Classifier: Rise-time based categorisation of a received signal
    with a symbol on the RX line. Retired in v1.5.1; the RX line symbol
    was removed.

  - Calling Frequency Jump: F+9 tunes the current band's simplex calling
    frequency with the right step and modulation (2 m FM/SSB, 70 cm
    FM/SSB, MURS, FRS/GMRS, CB, Marine, Airband), toast "CALL FREQ". The
    press-again restore was retired in v1.5.1; since v1.5.1 the jump is
    saved like a keyed-in frequency and refuses memory channels with
    "FREQ ONLY".

  - Categorized Menu System: A 7-category menu (RECEIVE, TONE, TX, SCAN,
    CHANNEL, CONFIG, plus an UNLOCK category that appears only when the
    radio is unlocked) replacing the stock flat list of 50+ items; number
    keys jump straight to a category.

  - Context-Aware Status Line: The bottom line of the main screen changes
    with what the radio is doing: idle, receiving, transmitting or
    scanning.

  - Toast Notification System: A one-second centred popup on the main
    screen confirming every shortcut action.

  - RSSI EWMA Filter: Smoothing of signal strength readings against
    S-meter jitter. Retired in v1.5.1.

  - RSSI Histogram with Noise Floor Analysis: Learns the real idle noise
    floor on your frequency so the smart squelch knows where signals
    begin. Since v1.5.1 it listens only to the parked, idle receiver and
    lets old readings fade out.

  - Signal Quality Metrics: Rates signal stability as the Q figure on the
    RX line and the bars beside it in the status bar. Since v1.5.1 it is
    computed from the raw reading.

  - Side-Button Toast Feedback: SIDE1, SIDE2 and long-press MENU actions
    show the same one-second confirmation as the F-key shortcuts. No
    upstream fork gives any on-screen feedback for side-button actions.

  - Scan Rate Telemetry: A live channels-per-second readout on the
    SCANNING status line ("SCANNING 12c/s"). No upstream fork shows
    scanner throughput to the operator.

  - F-Key UX Hardening: A long press of F shows "LOCKED" or "UNLOCKED";
    F+3 shows "NO CHANNELS" when no channel is stored instead of a
    misleading "VFO MODE"; F+STAR refuses AM and USB with "FM ONLY"
    because tone scanning only works in FM; a long press of STAR shows
    "SCAN ON" or "SCAN OFF". Each gap is original to VUURWERK.

  - Flashlight Auto-Off Watchdog with Battery-Aware Tier: The flashlight
    turns itself off after 30 minutes, or 10 minutes when the battery
    shows two bars or fewer; changing the flashlight mode restarts the
    timer, and SOS is never cut off. The battery-aware tier and the SOS
    rule are original to VUURWERK; no other UV-K5 fork ships them.

  - CSS Scan Soft-Timeout Watchdog: A tone scan that finds nothing stops
    after 30 seconds with a double beep and "SCAN FAIL." instead of
    running for ever (limit tightened to 30 seconds in v1.2.7, paired with
    a "NO SIGNAL" refusal on dead air and a faster lock). Original to
    VUURWERK; no other UV-K5 fork ships a scan watchdog with an operator
    cue.

  - Audio Palette Voice-Seek Cues: A short ascending chord confirms when a
    seek in the VOX spectrum lands on a voice. Sound means only "voice
    acquired"; the earlier no-voice cue was retired in v1.3.5. Original
    sound design gated on VUURWERK's voice scoring; no other UV-K5 fork
    has voice-gated audio cues because none has voice scoring.

  - Boot-Time Hardware Health Probe: At power-on the radio checks that the
    transceiver answers and that the battery calibration is present, and
    shows "BK4819 FAULT / RX/TX disabled" or "EEPROM FAULT / calib lost"
    instead of the welcome banner if not. Since v1.5.1 the fault banner
    shows even when the boot screen is set to NONE. Genuinely novel for
    the UV-K5 platform; no upstream fork ships any boot-time hardware
    diagnostic.

  - Live Battery Voltage During TX: The battery icon and voltage or
    percent text keep updating while you transmit, closing a staleness
    gap inherited by every upstream UV-K5 fork. Original to VUURWERK.

  - Backlight Fade-Out Tail and TX/RX Activity Refresh: The backlight
    fades over about 2 seconds instead of cutting to black, and stays lit
    while you talk or listen when BltTRX says so. The per-mode
    arm-and-hold behaviour is original to VUURWERK.

  - TX Battery Sag Delta Tracker: Measures how far the battery voltage
    dips during each transmission, shows it as "TX sag" on the About
    screen, and (v1.3.1) warns with a "SAG 0.NNV" toast after unkey when
    the dip reaches 0.20 V, the sign of a tired pack. Per-transmission
    battery accounting is original to VUURWERK.

  - CSS Scan Status-Bar Glyph and FOUND Beep: A "Cs" status-bar glyph
    marks a running tone scan, and a single beep confirms when the scan
    locks onto a tone; since v1.5.1 the menu tone scan and the F+STAR
    scan both confirm a lock. No upstream fork distinguishes tone scans
    visually or emits a found cue.

  - Quiet Backlight PWM: The backlight dimmer runs above the audio band so
    no whine reaches your transmitted audio when the screen is dimmed
    while you transmit. Technique credited to kamilsss655 (NUNU), who
    bench-validated it in 2024; VUURWERK's contribution is its integration
    (v1.2.7, reworked v1.5.1). Acknowledged as a port of an existing
    technique with original integration.

